01:35:30,539 --> 01:35:37,739
 It's a very important evaluation because if we care about equal pay for equal work,

526
01:35:38,079 --> 01:35:45,119
 and if we're not able to increase the rate of female management, it won't be as effective.

527
01:35:45,520 --> 01:35:48,739
 In the interest of time, we now give the floor back to our reviewers.

528
01:35:49,739 --> 01:35:58,479
 Thank you very much for your very comprehensive answers to my questions.

529
01:35:59,479 --> 01:36:14,619
 I want one issue to be clarified, because when we yesterday discussed the issue of introducing a comprehensive act on discrimination,

530
01:36:14,619 --> 01:36:29,939
 and then we understood that one of the problems with the preparation of the act was how to deal with disabled people with disabilities,

531
01:36:31,220 --> 01:36:39,579
 and the demand that there should be adjustments for these people.

532
01:36:39,579 --> 01:36:48,079
 So now I understood that actually in the present legislation on employment and discrimination,

533
01:36:49,760 --> 01:37:01,680
 there already is regarding employment a requirement that the employer should do these reasonable workplace adjustments,

534
01:37:01,859 --> 01:37:06,100
 otherwise it is discrimination. That's how I understood it.

535
01:37:06,100 --> 01:37:17,880
 And then my question is, are there any cases where employers have been punished for having not done these adjustments?

536
01:37:18,279 --> 01:37:26,520
 Is this also a more theoretical issue, or is it working also in practice? Thank you very much.

537
01:37:28,100 --> 01:37:33,119
 Thank you. I also have a question after you.

538
01:37:34,119 --> 01:37:41,399
 Thank you. Listening to the explanation provided by the Ministry of Labor,

539
01:37:43,060 --> 01:37:47,920
 I consistently heard equal pay for equal work.

540
01:37:48,939 --> 01:37:58,079
 And the question was whether you apply the concept of equal pay for work of equal value.

541
01:37:58,079 --> 01:38:05,180
 And when you explain to the companies the concept, do you also include this issue?

542
01:38:05,380 --> 01:38:21,260
 It's easy that you try to guarantee equal pay for the same work for female teachers, male teachers, female judges, male judges.

543
01:38:21,399 --> 01:38:25,359
 It's easy to understand, and nobody should be discriminated.

544
01:38:25,359 --> 01:38:34,119
 But what he raised is equal pay for work of equal value when the work is completely different,

545
01:38:34,600 --> 01:38:41,539
 but you have to estimate what kind of responsibility, what level of skill is needed.

546
01:38:42,159 --> 01:38:48,500
 So it might be completely different work, but might have same value.

547
01:38:48,500 --> 01:38:57,720
 So it needs government's effort to establish that kind of comparison, which work has the same value.

548
01:38:58,100 --> 01:39:03,420
 And we need to give equal pay for work of equal value.

549
01:39:03,539 --> 01:39:05,779
 That's what the convention means.

550
01:39:06,119 --> 01:39:15,699
 So I want to ask you whether when you understand this concept, do you have this understanding of equal pay for work of equal value?

551
01:39:15,699 --> 01:39:17,340
 That would be my question.

552
01:39:17,819 --> 01:39:18,239
 Thank you.

553
01:39:18,819 --> 01:39:19,699
 Thank you very much.

554
01:39:20,220 --> 01:39:27,720
 I think in addition to that, there's an overarching question of computation.

555
01:39:28,100 --> 01:39:37,380
 Have you taken the effort to calculate the unpaid care work economy in Taiwan?

556
01:39:37,920 --> 01:39:44,119
 Because until you have that baseline and you know the size of your unpaid care work,

557
01:39:44,119 --> 01:39:51,680
 then valuing work in different sectors becomes not completely objective.

558
01:39:52,239 --> 01:40:03,859
 And your national accounting system is missing in certain areas because work that is unpaid is not integrated into your economic planning.

559
01:40:04,340 --> 01:40:10,020
 And so the question is, in determining this equal work of equal value,

560
01:40:10,020 --> 01:40:17,939
 do you have a baseline that says in the formal, in the informal sector, this is the level of unpaid care,

561
01:40:18,239 --> 01:40:25,079
 this is the level of paid care so that the size of your labor market and your economy is clear?

562
01:40:25,359 --> 01:40:33,699
 Because this has also implications for the gender tax gap and which impacts also on gender pension gaps.

563
01:40:33,699 --> 01:40:38,779
 So if you are not looking at it as a spectrum, you know, as a continuum,

564
01:40:39,239 --> 01:40:46,039
 and you just start by saying, oh, yes, we have two, a man and a woman working, they're spending eight hours a day,

565
01:40:46,300 --> 01:40:55,720
 eight hours of cleaning and washing and sweeping may not be the same as eight hours of a telephone receptionist.

566
01:40:55,720 --> 01:41:04,140
 So valuing the work also means you have to expand your understanding of the care economy,

567
01:41:04,539 --> 01:41:09,000
 the formal economy, the enterprise economy, the market economy.

568
01:41:09,359 --> 01:41:11,699
 All of those make up your system.

569
01:41:11,979 --> 01:41:19,840
 So do you have that baseline is a question that I would like to put to the government because you are undertaking reforms,

570
01:41:19,840 --> 01:41:22,560
 but against what background?

571
01:41:22,800 --> 01:41:34,300
 Because if you don't have this data, then it's difficult to be able to assess how your progress is making an impact and closing gender gaps.

572
01:41:34,640 --> 01:41:42,680
 And so I would like to turn to the delegation maybe for quick answers because we still have Article 12,

573
01:41:42,680 --> 01:41:45,279
 where you have it and where you don't.

574
01:41:45,380 --> 01:41:50,539
 And we thank you for the very extensive responses that we've received this morning.

